{
  "image_name": "42742f94522dbc24a47f2cdebd56fe73.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes battery recycling pathways from whole packs down to separated electrode materials, contrasting today’s industrial practice with more selective, laboratory-scale approaches. It begins with received batteries being tested and triaged to decide whether modules can be reused, remanufactured, or sent to recycling. In common industrial routes, batteries are discharged and usually enter comminution, where packs or modules are manually opened only as needed and then shredded. Because manual disassembly is risky and labor intensive, most packs are reduced to smaller units before shredding, sometimes after thermal pretreatment or cooling measures to limit fire risk.\n\nShredding produces mixed outputs that are then physically separated into streams such as electrolyte, polymer-rich shreds, mixed black mass, and copper and aluminum fractions. Advanced separation can further refine these materials.\n\nAlongside this, the figure highlights a more targeted disassembly route. If cell type and pack configuration are known, packs can be dismantled into modules and cells, and cells can be split into anode, separator, and cathode layers. Binder removal and delamination then allow recovery of cleaner anode and cathode materials and metal foils. The overall message is that current recycling is dominated by shredding, while better pack design, data access, and automation could enable cleaner, higher-value material recovery.",
